/**
 * @page ApplicationArchitecture Application Architecture
 *
 * @brief Overview of the software and communication architecture.
 *
 * This page describes the interaction between the sensor module,
 * payload encoder, LoRaWAN communication module and The Things Stack.
 *
 * @section DesignChoices Design Choices
 *
 * @subsection PrototypeDesign Prototype Design Choices
 *
 * The prototype was designed to validate the complete LoRaWAN
 * communication chain before integrating physical sensors.
 *
 * The following design choices were made:
 *
 * - The HAN IoT Node was selected because it integrates the
 *   required microcontroller and LoRaWAN transceiver.
 *
 * - OTAA activation was selected because it provides improved
 *   security compared to ABP activation.
 *
 * - Potentiometers and push buttons were used instead of
 *   physical sensors to simplify testing and enable repeatable
 *   demonstrations.
 *
 * - A payload encoder is used to minimize payload size and
 *   airtime consumption.
 *
 *
 * @subsection FunctionalArchitecture Functional Architecture
 *
 * Figure 1 illustrates the interaction between the sensor module,
 * payload encoder, communication module and the main application.
 *
 * The main application collects sensor data, forwards the data
 * to the payload encoder and initiates LoRaWAN transmission.
 *
 * @dot "Functional Architecture"
 * digraph RainBarrelNode
 * {
 *   rankdir=LR;
 *
 *   Flow     [label="Flow Meter"];
 *   Level    [label="Level Meter"];
 *   Battery  [label="Battery Voltage"];
 *
 *   Sensors  [label="Sensor Module"];
 *
 *   MainLoop [label="Main Application\nloop()"];
 *
 *   Encoder  [label="Payload Encoder"];
 *
 *   TTN      [label="TTN Module"];
 *
 *   Gateway  [label="LoRaWAN Gateway"];
 *
 *   Flow -> Sensors;
 *   Level -> Sensors;
 *   Battery -> Sensors;
 *
 *   Sensors -> MainLoop;
 *
 *   MainLoop -> Encoder;
 *   Encoder -> TTN;
 *   TTN -> Gateway;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 *
 *
 * @subsection LoRaWANArchitecture LoRaWAN Communication Architecture
 *
 * Figure 2 shows the communication path between the prototype
 * node and the end user through the LoRaWAN infrastructure.
 *
 * @dot "LoRaWAN Communication Architecture"
 * digraph LoRaWANSystem
 * {
 *   rankdir=LR;
 *
 *   Sensors [label="Sensor Module"];
 *   Encoder [label="Payload Encoder"];
 *   Node    [label="LoRaWAN Node"];
 *   Gateway [label="LoRaWAN Gateway"];
 *   TTN     [label="The Things Stack"];
 *   User    [label="Private Home Owner"];
 *
 *   Sensors -> Encoder;
 *   Encoder -> Node;
 *   Node -> Gateway;
 *   Gateway -> TTN;
 *   TTN -> User;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 *
 *
 *
 * @section CommunicationDesign Communication Design
 *
 * @subsection TransmissionInterval Transmission Interval Selection
 * 
 * @note The calculations below are based on the LoRaWAN Airtime Calculator
 * and metadata collected from the prototype during testing.
 *
 * The uplink interval is configured to 5 minutes. This value was
 * selected based on the LoRaWAN airtime requirements of the device,
 * regulatory duty-cycle limitations in the EU868 frequency band,
 * and The Things Network Fair Access Policy.
 *
 * The prototype transmits a payload of 7 bytes. Including the LoRaWAN
 * protocol overhead (approximately 13 bytes), the resulting packet
 * occupies an airtime of approximately 102.9 ms when transmitted using
 * Data Rate 4 (DR4), corresponding to Spreading Factor 8 (SF8) and
 * a bandwidth of 125 kHz.
 *
 * Under the EU868 1% duty-cycle limitation, a transmission with an
 * airtime of 102.9 ms requires a silent period of approximately
 * 10.3 seconds before another transmission may occur. In theory this
 * would allow up to 349 messages per hour.
 *
 * However, The Things Network applies a Fair Access Policy that limits
 * the average airtime consumption of an end device. For the selected
 * transmission parameters, this corresponds to approximately:
 * - 12 uplink messages per hour
 * - 291 uplink messages per day
 * - approximately 4.9 minutes between consecutive uplinks
 *
 * Based on these constraints, a reporting interval of 5 minutes was
 * selected. This interval complies with network usage guidelines while
 * still providing timely information about the state of the rainwater
 * buffering system.
 *
 * The monitored parameters, water level and battery voltage, change
 * relatively slowly and therefore do not require high-frequency
 * reporting. In addition to the periodic transmission interval,
 * event-driven uplinks are generated when:
 *
 * - The battery voltage reaches 4.0 V (400 cV).
 * - The water level reaches 145 cm.
 *
 * This hybrid strategy combines scheduled monitoring with immediate
 * notification of significant events, ensuring efficient network usage
 * while maintaining awareness of critical operating conditions.
 *
 *
 *
 * @subsection DecisionLogic Transmission Decision Logic
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph DecisionFlow
 * {
 *   rankdir=TB;
 *
 *   Start   [shape=ellipse,label="Read Sensors"];
 *
 *   Battery [shape=diamond,label="battery_cV == 400?"];
 *   Level   [shape=diamond,label="level_cM == 145?"];
 *   Timer   [shape=diamond,label="5 minutes elapsed?"];
 *
 *   Send    [shape=box,label="Build and Send Uplink"];
 *   Wait    [shape=box,label="Continue Monitoring"];
 *
 *   Start -> Battery;
 *
 *   Battery -> Send [label="Yes"];
 *   Battery -> Level [label="No"];
 *
 *   Level -> Send [label="Yes"];
 *   Level -> Timer [label="No"];
 *
 *   Timer -> Send [label="Yes"];
 *   Timer -> Wait [label="No"];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 *
 * @see ShieldLayout
 *
 */